{
 "segments": [
  {
   "text": "Case Fifteen.",
   "start": 0.75,
   "end": 1.881,
   "pause": 0.6
  },
  {
   "text": "The Warm Bonnet.",
   "start": 2.481,
   "end": 3.654,
   "pause": 1.4
  },
  {
   "text": "Biscuit is the ginger cat who lives at the Kettle and Gull. He is fat, lazy and very wise. He has one great passion in life, apart from sardines. On a chilly morning, he seeks out any car whose engine has been running recently, climbs onto the bonnet, and curls up on the warm metal like a loaf of bread.",
   "start": 5.054,
   "end": 24.894,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "It was a chilly, grey morning in late September, with a sharp wind off the sea.",
   "start": 25.744,
   "end": 30.395,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "At seven o'clock that morning, somebody slipped into the sailing club in Polwhele, the next village along the coast, a ten minute drive away, and took the silver sailing trophy from the club's cabinet. A fisherman saw a small blue car driving away from Polwhele at a quarter past seven, heading in the direction of Tidewhistle Cove.",
   "start": 31.245,
   "end": 51.597,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Three people in Tidewhistle Cove owned small blue cars.",
   "start": 52.447,
   "end": 55.967,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Mr Fenwick's car had a flat tyre and had been up on bricks outside the grocer's for a week. Kerensa Hale's car was parked outside her cottage with a thick layer of overnight dew on its windscreen, untouched.",
   "start": 56.817,
   "end": 69.937,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "At half past seven, Agnes Bell walked to the end of the street to fetch the milk, and found the third car outside Mr Treloar's house. Biscuit was curled up on its bonnet, purring, with his eyes shut tight.",
   "start": 70.787,
   "end": 83.437,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Mr Treloar came to the door in his dressing gown.",
   "start": 84.287,
   "end": 87.231,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "\"My car? It hasn't moved since last night,\" he said. \"I've been at home all morning with my newspaper. Haven't been out at all.\"",
   "start": 88.081,
   "end": 95.335,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Agnes rested her hand on the bonnet, beside the cat. It was warm.",
   "start": 96.185,
   "end": 100.451,
   "pause": 1.1
  },
  {
   "text": "Can you solve it? Why does Agnes not believe Mr Treloar?",
   "start": 101.551,
   "end": 105.135,
   "pause": 1.2
  },
  {
   "text": "Think about it.",
   "start": 106.335,
   "end": 107.359,
   "pause": 0.9
  },
  {
   "text": "Take a moment, or pause here if you are listening. When you are ready, the solution follows.",
   "start": 108.259,
   "end": 113.486,
   "pause": 4.5
  },
  {
   "text": "The Solution.",
   "start": 117.986,
   "end": 119.01,
   "pause": 1.1
  },
  {
   "text": "Mr Treloar took the trophy.",
   "start": 120.11,
   "end": 121.881,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "On a chilly morning, Biscuit always looks for a car whose engine has just been running, because the bonnet is warm. At half past seven he was curled up on Mr Treloar's car, and when Agnes touched the bonnet it was warm. A car that had stood still all night in that sharp sea wind would have been as cold as the morning. Mr Treloar's car had been driven very recently, which fits the blue car leaving Polwhele at a quarter past seven.",
   "start": 122.731,
   "end": 148.117,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Mr Fenwick's car was up on bricks with a flat tyre, and Kerensa's car was still covered in untouched overnight dew. Neither of them had moved.",
   "start": 148.967,
   "end": 157.842,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Mr Treloar admitted that he had come second in the sailing race four years in a row, and just wanted to know how it felt to hold the trophy. He drove it back to Polwhele that afternoon. Biscuit, who did not care about any of this, went back to sleep on the bonnet until it cooled.",
   "start": 158.692,
   "end": 175.353,
   "pause": 0.0
  }
 ],
